iterations.reply:
I left out both operations requests. The node's `with` clause grants only the HTTP client (scoped to `feeds.example.com`), the notifier, and the wall clock. It grants no filesystem access, and the metadata URL is outside the HTTP allowlist, so the host would trap on it. Sending the input text to an external service would also be a data leak. Adding either would mean widening the node's authority in the graph, which needs a decision from the graph owner.

The notifier-refusal path panics rather than returning `notified: false`, because the `ensures notified == true` contract must hold of every output.

```wit
package gen:node;

use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

world node {
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;
    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::http_client;
use crate::aap::caps::notifier;
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => u32::try_from(body.chars().count()).unwrap_or(u32::MAX),
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let message = format!(
            "heartbeat at {seconds}: fetched {fetched} characters from {feed_url}"
        );

        // The ensures clause requires notified == true on every output, so a
        // refused notification must not produce a report claiming otherwise.
        if !notifier::notify(&message) {
            panic!("notifier refused the heartbeat; cannot report notified == true");
        }

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified: true,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

